BANK.push(
{"id":"g1","d":1,"q":"A multinational retailer is consolidating customer data from its EU, Brazilian, and US subsidiaries into a single analytics platform hosted in a US region of a public cloud provider. The legal team has confirmed that the EU data includes purchase histories linked to loyalty IDs. The business wants the platform live within six months. As the security architect, what should you do FIRST?","o":["Identify the legal and regulatory requirements that apply to each data set, including transfer restrictions, and turn them into architecture requirements","Encrypt all data at rest in the US region using customer-managed keys, so that the cloud provider and its staff can never access the EU residents' records","Pseudonymize the loyalty IDs inside each region before the data leaves, so the consolidated data set is no longer considered personal data under any of the three laws","Negotiate a data processing agreement with the cloud provider that incorporates the EU standard contractual clauses before any data is migrated to the platform"],"a":[0],"e":"Requirements come before controls. Until you know which laws apply (GDPR, LGPD, US state laws) and what they require for transfer, retention, and data subject rights, you can't pick the right controls. Encryption, pseudonymization, and SCCs may all end up in the design, but each is a solution to a requirement not yet defined. Note too that pseudonymized data is still personal data under GDPR."},
{"id":"g2","d":1,"q":"An organization's risk register shows a residual risk rated 'high' for a legacy payment application that cannot be patched until it is replaced in 18 months. Compensating controls (network isolation and enhanced monitoring) are already in place. The CIO asks the security architect to 'close out' the risk item before the board meeting. What is the MOST appropriate action?","o":["Document the residual risk and have a business owner with the right authority formally accept it, with a review date tied to the replacement","Downgrade the risk rating to medium, since network isolation and enhanced monitoring already reduce the likelihood of exploitation significantly","Transfer the risk by purchasing a cyber insurance policy that specifically covers payment card breaches and the associated PCI fines","Remove the item from the register, because the replacement project is funded and the risk will disappear when the new system goes live"],"a":[0],"e":"A residual risk above appetite must be accepted by someone with the authority to do so, documented, and revisited. Changing the rating without new analysis is manipulation, insurance doesn't remove PCI obligations or reputational damage, and removing an open risk hides it from governance."},
{"id":"g3","d":1,"q":"A hospital is designing a new telehealth platform that will be operated by a third-party SaaS vendor. The vendor will store recordings of patient consultations. The hospital's legal counsel confirms that the hospital remains accountable for the protection of the data. Which architecture artifact BEST ensures that the hospital's security requirements are enforceable against the vendor?","o":["Security requirements written into the contract and BAA, with right-to-audit and breach notice","The vendor's SOC 2 Type II report, reviewed and accepted by the hospital's security team during the procurement process","A shared responsibility matrix, maintained by the hospital's security team and reviewed with the vendor at every quarterly business review","An internal hospital policy requiring that every vendor follow the hospital's own information security standards and procedures"],"a":[0],"e":"Only contractual terms are enforceable against a third party. A SOC 2 report gives assurance about the past period but creates no obligations, a responsibility matrix is useful but has no legal force on its own, and internal policy doesn't bind a vendor."},
{"id":"g4","d":1,"q":"During a design review for a new data lake, the architect notices that the business case lists 'retain all raw data indefinitely for future machine learning use.' The data includes personal data of EU residents collected for order fulfillment. Which privacy principle does this requirement MOST directly conflict with?","o":["Storage limitation and purpose limitation, because the data is kept indefinitely for new, undefined purposes beyond fulfillment","Accuracy, because raw historical data becomes stale and may no longer reflect the customer's current details over time","Integrity and confidentiality, because a data lake concentrates personal data and increases the impact of any breach","Lawfulness, fairness, and transparency, because customers were not told that their data would be used for machine learning"],"a":[0],"e":"Keeping personal data indefinitely for undefined future purposes conflicts with storage limitation (keep no longer than needed) and purpose limitation (collected for fulfillment, not open-ended ML). Accuracy and integrity aren't the core issue."},
{"id":"g5","d":1,"q":"A security architect is asked to align the enterprise security program with a framework that the board can use to see current maturity and target state across Identify, Protect, Detect, Respond, Recover, and Govern functions. Which framework BEST fits this need?","o":["NIST CSF 2.0 profiles","ISO/IEC 27002, using its control catalog organized into organizational, people, physical, and technological themes","PCI DSS v4.0, using its twelve requirements and the customized approach to show maturity across the security program","COBIT 2019, using its process capability levels to show the board current and target maturity for each governance objective"],"a":[0],"e":"NIST CSF 2.0 is organized around the six functions named (Govern was added in 2.0) and uses profiles to express current and target state, which suits board reporting. ISO 27002 is a control catalog, PCI DSS is a compliance standard for card data, and COBIT is broader IT governance."},
{"id":"g6","d":1,"q":"An organization runs the same e-commerce platform in three countries. Country A requires that citizens' personal data be stored only within national borders. Countries B and C have no such rule. The business wants a single global database to reduce costs. What is the BEST architectural response?","o":["Partition the data so Country A's records stay in an in-country store, with the global platform using controlled interfaces","Keep a single global database and encrypt Country A's records with a key that is generated and held only by a key manager in Country A","Store all three countries' data in Country A, since that satisfies the strictest requirement and keeps a single database for the platform","Ask Country A's regulator for an exemption, on the grounds that a single global database is cheaper and easier to secure consistently"],"a":[0],"e":"Data residency rules usually concern where data is stored, and many regulators don't accept encryption with a local key as a substitute. Partitioning meets the law while keeping the global design for the rest. Moving everything to Country A may create new problems (performance, other laws) and isn't needed."},
{"id":"g7","d":1,"q":"A company's board has approved a risk appetite statement saying the organization has 'low appetite for risks that could cause regulatory sanction.' A project team proposes launching a new product with a known gap in consent capture that legal believes has a 30% chance of a regulator inquiry. What should the security architect recommend?","o":["Escalate it through governance with options","Approve the launch, because a 30% likelihood is below the 50% threshold that most organizations use to define a 'likely' risk event","Block the launch on the architect's own authority, since the board's appetite statement already makes the answer clear","Accept the risk on behalf of the project so the team can meet its launch date, and record the acceptance in the risk register"],"a":[0],"e":"The architect advises; they don't own business risk decisions. The appetite statement says this risk is likely outside tolerance, so it must go to the people who can decide, with options. Architects shouldn't unilaterally block or accept, and 'below 50%' isn't a meaningful threshold."},
{"id":"g8","d":1,"q":"An organization is subject to both PCI DSS and SOX. The audit team complains that they test the same access control on the ERP system three separate times for different frameworks. What architectural approach BEST addresses this?","o":["Build a unified control framework that maps each control to every applicable requirement, so it is tested once and reported many times","Split the ERP into two separate instances, one inside PCI scope and one for SOX financial reporting, so each audit covers only its own system","Adopt whichever framework is stricter and stop testing against the other, since meeting the stricter one automatically satisfies both","Give each framework's audit to a different external firm so that the testing workload is spread more evenly across the year"],"a":[0],"e":"A common control framework maps controls to all applicable requirements. Testing once and reusing evidence cuts cost without lowering assurance. Splitting systems adds complexity, and you can't drop a legal obligation because another framework is stricter."},
{"id":"g9","d":1,"q":"A security architect performs a quantitative risk analysis. A data center has an asset value of $4,000,000. A flood would destroy 25% of it. Floods are expected once every 20 years. A flood barrier costs $30,000 per year to maintain and reduces the exposure factor to 5%. What is the annual value of the safeguard to the organization?","o":["$10,000","$40,000","$50,000","-$20,000"],"a":[0],"e":"SLE before = 4,000,000 x 0.25 = 1,000,000; ARO = 1/20 = 0.05; ALE before = 50,000. SLE after = 4,000,000 x 0.05 = 200,000; ALE after = 10,000. Safeguard value = ALE before - ALE after - annual cost = 50,000 - 10,000 - 30,000 = $10,000. $40,000 forgets the cost of the control, and $50,000 is the original ALE."},
{"id":"g10","d":1,"q":"After a merger, two companies have conflicting information security policies. Company A requires 15-character passwords with no expiry; Company B requires 8-character passwords rotated every 60 days. The CISO asks the architect to propose a harmonized approach. Which is the BEST basis for the decision?","o":["Current authoritative guidance plus the combined risk assessment, such as NIST SP 800-63B's focus on length and breach screening","Whichever existing policy covers the larger number of employees, since that minimizes the amount of change and retraining needed","Company B's policy, since frequent rotation limits how long a stolen password stays useful and is therefore always the more secure choice","Keep both policies in place indefinitely, with each legacy company following its own rules, to avoid disrupting users after the merger"],"a":[0],"e":"Policy should rest on risk and current guidance. NIST 800-63B favors longer passwords, breached-password screening, and no periodic forced rotation without evidence of compromise. Headcount isn't a risk basis, rotation isn't automatically better, and two policies undermine governance."},
{"id":"g11","d":1,"q":"A security architecture governance board wants to ensure that exceptions to security standards don't become permanent. Which control design is MOST effective?","o":["Time-bound exceptions with a named risk owner, compensating controls, and automatic re-review or expiry when the period ends","Require the CISO to personally review and sign every exception request before it can be approved by the board","Publish every approved exception on the corporate intranet so that all staff can see which teams have been granted one","Prohibit exceptions entirely, so every system must meet the security standard in full before it can go into production"],"a":[0],"e":"Expiry dates, ownership, and compensating controls keep exceptions visible and temporary. CISO signatures alone don't drive review, publishing doesn't enforce anything, and banning exceptions pushes teams to bypass the process."},
{"id":"g12","d":1,"q":"An organization plans to move its HR system to a SaaS provider. The provider offers a SOC 2 Type I report only. Procurement says this is 'good enough because it's a SOC 2.' What is the MOST important limitation the architect should raise?","o":["Type I only evaluates control design at a point in time; it does not test whether controls operated effectively over a period","Type I reports cover only controls relevant to financial reporting and do not address security, availability, or confidentiality","Type I reports are prepared by the service provider's own internal audit team rather than by an independent CPA firm","Type I reports are recognized only for providers outside the United States and can't be relied on for US-based services"],"a":[0],"e":"Type I attests to design at a point in time. Type II tests operating effectiveness over a period (often 6-12 months). Both are independent CPA attestations against the Trust Services Criteria; SOC 1 is the financial controls report."},
{"id":"g13","d":1,"q":"A manufacturer exports encryption-enabled industrial controllers to several countries. Engineering wants to embed AES-256 in the firmware and ship to all customers. Which consideration MUST the security architect raise before finalizing the design?","o":["Export control and import regulations covering cryptography in each destination country, which can legally block shipment","Whether the AES-256 implementation is FIPS 140-3 validated on the specific processor used in the controller","Whether customers have an internal PKI capable of issuing and managing the keys the controllers will need in the field","Whether AES-256 will remain secure once large quantum computers capable of running Grover's algorithm become available"],"a":[0],"e":"Cryptographic products are subject to export controls (for example, US EAR) and some countries restrict import or use. This is a legal requirement that can block shipping. FIPS validation and PKI are valid design questions but not legal blockers, and AES-256 is considered adequate against known quantum attacks."},
{"id":"g14","d":1,"q":"An organization's business impact analysis shows that its claims processing system has a maximum tolerable downtime of 48 hours. The current backup architecture restores the system in 72 hours. The business proposes accepting the gap because 'outages are rare.' What should the architect do?","o":["Present the gap as a risk with options and costs to close it, and have the business owner decide","Accept the gap without further action, since the business owner has already said that outages are rare enough to tolerate","Redesign the backup architecture to achieve a four-hour RTO so that the gap is closed regardless of the cost to the business","Update the BIA so that the claims system's maximum tolerable downtime is 72 hours, matching what the architecture can deliver today"],"a":[0],"e":"The gap means the business could exceed what it said it can survive. That must be a documented, owned risk decision with options. Silently accepting it, over-engineering it, or editing the BIA to match reality all bypass governance."},
{"id":"g15","d":1,"q":"A bank's architecture team must decide between two designs. Design A costs $2M and reduces annualized loss expectancy from $1.2M to $300K. Design B costs $500K and reduces it from $1.2M to $700K. Both have a five-year life with no ongoing costs. Based purely on cost-benefit, which is BETTER and why?","o":["Design A, by $500K of net benefit","Design B, because it costs a quarter as much as Design A while still reducing annualized losses by more than 40 percent","Design B, because its return on investment of five to one is more than twice Design A's ratio, so it delivers more net value","They are equivalent, because both designs bring the residual annualized loss expectancy below the original $1.2M figure"],"a":[0],"e":"Design A: 5 x (1.2M - 0.3M) = 4.5M benefit - 2M = 2.5M net. Design B: 5 x (1.2M - 0.7M) = 2.5M - 0.5M = 2.0M net. B has a better ratio (5:1 vs 2.25:1), but A delivers more total net value. 'Cheaper' isn't the same as 'better.'"},
{"id":"g16","d":1,"q":"Which of the following BEST describes the role of the security architect in the organization's risk management process?","o":["Translate business risk decisions into architecture designs, and advise owners on design risk","Own and formally accept all information security risks on behalf of the business, since the architect understands the technical risk best","Perform every risk assessment in the organization and set the risk appetite that business units must use when making decisions","Independently audit the effectiveness of security controls after they are implemented, and report the results to the board"],"a":[0],"e":"Architects advise and design. The business owns and accepts risk, the board sets appetite, and auditors provide independent assurance. Mixing audit with design undermines independence."},
{"id":"g17","d":1,"q":"A company uses a cloud provider's managed database service. A regulator requires the company to demonstrate that encryption keys for regulated data are under the company's exclusive control. Which design BEST satisfies this?","o":["An external key manager or customer-held HSM (hold-your-own-key), so the provider never holds or can use the key material","The provider's default encryption at rest, which uses provider-managed AES-256 keys rotated automatically every year","Customer-managed keys stored in the provider's key management service, with key policies that only the company can edit","Encryption of the database backups only, since the live database is already protected by the provider's access controls"],"a":[0],"e":"'Exclusive control' implies the provider can't access keys. Provider-managed keys fail outright. Customer-managed keys in the provider's KMS give control over policy and rotation, but the key material still sits in provider infrastructure. External key management or HYOK keeps key material outside the provider."},
{"id":"g18","d":1,"q":"An architect is asked to produce evidence for an upcoming ISO/IEC 27001 certification audit that the organization has selected controls based on risk. Which artifact is MOST directly required?","o":["A Statement of Applicability listing each Annex A control, whether it is included, and the justification for that decision","A current network diagram showing every firewall, segment, and trust boundary in the scope of the ISMS","A list of all security products purchased in the past year, mapped to the Annex A control each one supports","Penetration test reports from each of the past three years, with evidence that all findings were remediated"],"a":[0],"e":"ISO 27001 requires a Statement of Applicability that links risk treatment to the chosen Annex A controls and justifies inclusions and exclusions. The others may support the audit but aren't the core required artifact."},
{"id":"g19","d":1,"q":"A global company wants to standardize data classification. Business units currently use different labels ('Confidential,' 'Restricted,' 'Internal Only,' 'Secret'). Which step should come FIRST in designing a unified scheme?","o":["Define levels by the business impact of disclosure, modification, or loss, with clear criteria and owners","Deploy a DLP tool that automatically labels all documents, then adjust the label names based on what the tool finds","Adopt a government scheme such as Top Secret, Secret, and Confidential, since it is well understood and widely documented","Ask each business unit to rename its existing labels to match those used by the largest unit, to minimize disruption"],"a":[0],"e":"Classification must be grounded in impact criteria and data ownership. Tools enforce a scheme; they don't define it. Government schemes fit government needs, not commercial risk, and renaming labels without criteria doesn't create consistency."},
{"id":"g20","d":1,"q":"A software vendor supplies a critical component to an organization's supply chain. A risk assessment finds the vendor has no secure development program. The vendor is the only supplier available. Which risk treatment is MOST appropriate?","o":["Mitigate through contract, testing, and isolation","Avoid the risk by refusing to use the vendor, even though no alternative supplier exists for the critical component","Accept the risk without further action, because there is no alternative supplier and the component is required","Transfer the risk entirely to the vendor through an indemnification clause that covers any breach the component causes"],"a":[0],"e":"With no alternative, avoidance isn't practical, and accepting without action ignores reasonable mitigations. Indemnification shifts some financial impact but not operational or reputational damage, so risk can't be fully transferred. Layered mitigation is the best choice."},
{"id":"g21","d":1,"q":"A security architect is preparing a design for a system that will process California residents' personal information and sell aggregated insights to partners. Which requirement MUST the architecture support?","o":["Let consumers opt out of the sale or sharing of their personal information, and honor that preference downstream","Encrypt all personal information of California residents with AES-256, both at rest and in transit between partners","Store all personal information of California residents in data centers physically located within the state","Commission an annual penetration test from a firm on the state's list of approved security assessors"],"a":[0],"e":"CCPA/CPRA gives consumers the right to opt out of sale or sharing, including via signals such as Global Privacy Control. The architecture must capture and propagate that preference. The law has no residency or state-approved-testing requirement and doesn't mandate a specific algorithm."},
{"id":"g22","d":1,"q":"An organization is deciding whether to build a security capability in-house or buy a managed service. Which factor is MOST important from a governance perspective?","o":["Whether the organization can still meet its accountability obligations and oversee the outsourced function","Which option has the lowest total cost in the first year, including setup, licensing, and staffing costs","Whether the managed service uses newer detection technology than the organization could build on its own","Whether the managed service provider is headquartered locally, so that staff can visit the provider's facility easily"],"a":[0],"e":"You can outsource the function but not the accountability. Governance asks whether oversight, reporting, and contractual controls let you meet obligations. Cost and technology matter but are secondary."},
{"id":"g23","d":1,"q":"During a risk assessment workshop, stakeholders disagree strongly on the likelihood of a ransomware attack, with estimates ranging from 'once in ten years' to 'twice a year.' The organization has little historical data. Which approach BEST improves the quality of the estimate?","o":["Structured expert elicitation, such as calibrated estimates or a Delphi technique, informed by industry threat data","Take the average of the highest and lowest estimates, so that the final figure reflects every stakeholder's view","Use the highest estimate, since a conservative assumption is safest when there is little historical data available","Defer the assessment until the organization has collected enough internal incident data to calculate a reliable rate"],"a":[0],"e":"Structured elicitation reduces bias and anchors on external data such as industry reports. Averaging extremes isn't analysis, always taking the worst case distorts priorities, and waiting for incidents to build data defeats the purpose."},
{"id":"g24","d":1,"q":"A company is acquiring a smaller firm whose network will be connected to the parent within 90 days. Due diligence found unpatched systems and no asset inventory at the target. What should the security architect recommend FIRST?","o":["Treat the acquired network as untrusted, connect it through controlled interfaces, and require an inventory","Connect the networks right away so that the parent company's scanning and monitoring tools can start assessing the target's systems","Rebuild every server and workstation at the acquired firm to the parent's standard build before allowing any network connection","Recommend delaying the acquisition until the target firm achieves ISO 27001 certification for its entire environment"],"a":[0],"e":"Untrusted-until-assessed integration limits the blast radius while giving visibility. Immediate full connection spreads risk, rebuilding everything may be impractical, and the architect doesn't control the acquisition timeline."},
{"id":"g25","d":1,"q":"A regulator issues a new rule requiring financial institutions to report material cyber incidents within 72 hours. Which architectural capability is MOST critical to meeting this requirement?","o":["Detection and triage processes that determine materiality quickly and route it to accountable executives","Immutable backups for all critical systems, so that the institution can recover quickly before the reporting deadline","A dedicated, isolated network segment for the incident response team to work from during a material incident","End-to-end encryption of all incident response communications, so that attackers can't monitor the response"],"a":[0],"e":"You can't report what you haven't detected and assessed. The 72-hour clock depends on fast detection, impact assessment, and a clear decision path. Backups and secure comms support response but don't drive the reporting deadline."},
{"id":"g26","d":1,"q":"A security architect is building a business case for a new privileged access management platform. Executives keep asking 'what do we get for this money?' Which approach will MOST effectively communicate value to them?","o":["Tie the investment to the business risks it reduces, in business terms such as expected loss avoided and initiatives enabled","Present the number of unmanaged privileged accounts found during the pilot scan, to show how large the exposure currently is","Present a feature-by-feature comparison of the PAM platform against its main competitors to show it is the best technical choice","Cite independent analyst rankings that place the chosen PAM platform as a market leader, to reassure executives about the choice"],"a":[0],"e":"Executives fund risk reduction and business enablement, not features. Account counts and analyst quadrants may support the case but don't answer 'what does the business get.'"},
{"id":"g27","d":1,"q":"An organization stores backup tapes with a third-party vault provider. A new regulation requires that personal data be erasable on request within 30 days. Restoring and editing tapes is impractical. Which approach BEST meets the requirement while keeping backups useful?","o":["Crypto-shredding of per-subject keys","Recall all tapes from the vault every month and degauss them, then take fresh backups that exclude erased data subjects","Stop taking backups of any system that contains personal data, and rely on database replication for recovery instead","Inform data subjects that backup media are exempt from erasure requests under the regulation and need not be changed"],"a":[0],"e":"Crypto-shredding makes targeted data unrecoverable without touching the media. Regulators often accept 'beyond use' approaches for backups when documented. Destroying all backups or stopping them harms availability, and claiming a blanket exemption isn't defensible."},
{"id":"g28","d":1,"q":"A company's legal department is preparing for possible litigation over a product defect. They issue a legal hold covering all engineering communications from the last five years. The current email architecture automatically deletes messages older than three years. What must the architecture support?","o":["Suspend automated deletion for in-scope custodians and data, preserving integrity and chain of custody","Export all email immediately to an external e-discovery vendor and remove it from the company's systems","Delete all engineering communications older than five years to limit the company's legal exposure","Encrypt all held messages with a key that only the legal department can access, then resume normal deletion"],"a":[0],"e":"Legal holds override retention schedules for in-scope data. Deleting relevant data after a hold is spoliation and can lead to sanctions. Exporting everything is excessive, and deleting to 'limit exposure' is unlawful once litigation is anticipated."},
{"id":"g29","d":1,"q":"A security architect is designing controls for a payment processing environment. To reduce PCI DSS assessment scope, which architectural approach is MOST effective?","o":["Tokenize or use P2PE so that most systems never store, process, or transmit card data, and segment the systems that remain","Encrypt cardholder data in the database with AES-256, which takes the database and its servers out of PCI scope","Put all systems on one flat network protected by a strong perimeter firewall that meets PCI DSS requirement 1","Buy a firewall product that the vendor certifies as PCI compliant and place it between the CDE and the internet"],"a":[0],"e":"Scope is determined by where cardholder data flows and what can affect it. Tokenization and P2PE remove card data from most systems, and segmentation isolates the rest. Encrypted data is still in scope in most cases, flat networks pull everything into scope, and no product is 'PCI compliant' on its own."},
{"id":"g30","d":1,"q":"An international company must comply with GDPR. It plans to use a US-based SaaS provider for customer support. The provider is certified under the EU-US Data Privacy Framework. Which statement is MOST accurate?","o":["DPF provides a transfer mechanism, but a processing agreement and the controller's own assessment are still required","DPF certification means the provider is already bound by GDPR directly, so no further contract with the provider is needed","DPF certification is irrelevant, because GDPR prohibits any transfer of EU personal data to a provider based in the US","The company must also obtain explicit, separate consent from every customer before any data is transferred to the provider"],"a":[0],"e":"DPF addresses the cross-border transfer question only. Article 28 still requires a processor agreement, and the controller remains responsible for due diligence. Consent is one possible basis but isn't required when another valid mechanism exists."},
{"id":"g31","d":1,"q":"Which BEST describes 'due care' as it relates to a security architect's responsibilities?","o":["Implementing and operating reasonable controls that a prudent organization would apply to protect its assets","Researching threats, vulnerabilities, and suppliers before decisions are made, and verifying controls afterward","Purchasing insurance to cover the potential losses that remain after security controls have been put in place","Documenting every security decision and its rationale so that auditors can see how each decision was reached"],"a":[0],"e":"Due care is doing what a reasonable person would do: acting. Due diligence is the investigation and ongoing verification that informs and confirms that care. Both matter, but the question asks about due care."},
{"id":"g32","d":1,"q":"An architect is asked to design a third-party risk management program for 2,000 suppliers. Assessing each one in depth is impossible. Which design is MOST effective?","o":["Tier suppliers by inherent risk (data access, criticality, connectivity) and scale the depth of each assessment to match the tier","Send the same detailed 300-question questionnaire to every supplier each year, so all are assessed consistently","Assess only suppliers whose annual contract value exceeds a fixed dollar threshold, since they represent the most risk","Require every supplier to obtain ISO 27001 certification within a year as a condition of continuing the relationship"],"a":[0],"e":"Risk-based tiering focuses effort where the exposure is. A uniform questionnaire wastes effort and gets low-quality answers, spend doesn't equal risk (a cheap supplier can hold sensitive data), and blanket certification is unrealistic for small suppliers."},
{"id":"g33","d":1,"q":"A security architect discovers that a business unit has deployed a cloud application that processes regulated health data without involving security or legal. The application is critical to a revenue stream. What is the BEST first response?","o":["Assess it and bring it under governance","Shut down the application immediately, since processing regulated health data without approval is a compliance breach","Report the business unit leader to human resources for violating the organization's technology procurement policy","Take no action, because the business unit accepted the risk when it chose to deploy the application on its own"],"a":[0],"e":"Shadow IT must be brought under governance, but a measured, risk-based response protects the business. Shutting it down may cause more harm than the risk, and the business unit lacks authority to accept regulatory risk for the whole organization."},
{"id":"g34","d":1,"q":"A company's insurer requires multi-factor authentication for all remote access and email as a condition of renewing its cyber policy. The current VPN supports MFA but 15% of users have been exempted. What is the MOST significant risk of leaving the exemptions in place?","o":["The insurer could deny a claim for a related incident because the attested MFA control was not fully in place","Users with exemptions will experience slower VPN performance because their sessions are not optimized","The VPN vendor may revoke support for the deployment because MFA was not enabled on all accounts","The exempted accounts may cause the VPN concentrator to fail open during periods of high load"],"a":[0],"e":"Insurance applications are representations. If the organization attests to MFA everywhere and a breach occurs through an exempt account, the insurer may deny coverage. That turns a transferred risk back into a retained one."},
{"id":"g35","d":1,"q":"The board asks the CISO for a single metric to show whether the security program is reducing risk over time. Which is the MOST meaningful key risk indicator?","o":["Critical assets with risk above appetite","Total number of connections blocked by perimeter firewalls each month, trended over the last year","Number of security tools deployed across the enterprise, compared with the number a year earlier","Security spending as a percentage of the IT budget, compared with the industry average for peers"],"a":[0],"e":"Risk-oriented indicators tied to appetite tell the board whether exposure is shrinking. Blocked connections and tool counts measure activity, not risk, and spend is an input, not an outcome."},
{"id":"g36","d":1,"q":"An architect must decide where to host a new system that will process sensitive defense-related technical data controlled under ITAR. Which hosting requirement is MOST critical?","o":["Ensure that only authorized US persons can access or administer the data, which may require a government cloud with screened staff","Use any commercial cloud region in the United States, since ITAR is satisfied when data stays within US borders","Encrypt the data with AES-256 before storing it, so that the physical location and the provider's staff do not matter","Use an on-premises data center in any NATO member country, since allied nations are treated as US persons under ITAR"],"a":[0],"e":"ITAR restricts access by foreign persons, not just data location. A US commercial region may still have non-US-person support staff. Encryption can help in some cases, but access by provider personnel is the key issue and must be addressed explicitly."},
{"id":"g37","d":1,"q":"A company is drafting a security policy hierarchy. Which statement correctly describes the relationship between policies, standards, and procedures?","o":["Policies state intent; standards are mandatory rules; procedures give the steps","Procedures are approved by the board of directors, while policies are written and approved by technical staff","Standards are optional recommendations that teams may adopt, while guidelines are mandatory for all staff","Policies should contain specific technical settings, such as minimum password length, so they can be enforced"],"a":[0],"e":"Policy = what and why (management intent). Standards = mandatory specifics. Procedures = how, step by step. Guidelines are recommended, not mandatory. Putting technical settings in policy makes it brittle."},
{"id":"g38","d":1,"q":"A financial services firm is subject to a regulation requiring operational resilience: it must remain within impact tolerances for important business services during severe but plausible disruptions. Which architecture activity BEST addresses this?","o":["Map important business services end to end, including third parties, and test scenarios against tolerances","Increase the redundancy of the primary data center's core network so that a single device failure can't cause an outage","Perform disaster recovery tests of individual servers every year and record the results in the business continuity plan","Purchase business interruption insurance that covers the revenue lost during a severe but plausible disruption"],"a":[0],"e":"Operational resilience regulations (UK, EU DORA) focus on services, not systems. You must map dependencies and test against tolerances. Server-level DR tests and network redundancy are components but miss the end-to-end view."},
{"id":"g39","d":1,"q":"An organization is evaluating risk assessment approaches. It needs results it can compare directly with financial investments and wants to justify spending to the CFO. Which approach is MOST suitable?","o":["Quantitative analysis such as FAIR, expressing loss estimates in monetary terms with ranges","A qualitative heat map rating likelihood and impact as high, medium, or low for each risk","A checklist-based gap assessment of the security program against the ISO 27001 Annex A controls","Severity scores from the vulnerability scanner, aggregated by business unit and trended over time"],"a":[0],"e":"Quantitative methods like FAIR express risk as probable loss in dollars, which the CFO can compare with other investments. Heat maps are easy but not financial, gap assessments measure compliance, and CVSS measures technical severity, not business loss."},
{"id":"g40","d":1,"q":"A security architect is reviewing a contract with a cloud provider. The provider's standard terms limit its liability to the fees paid in the prior 12 months. The workload contains highly sensitive customer data. What should the architect advise?","o":["Recognize that most breach costs will remain with you, design controls accordingly, and negotiate where possible","Reject the provider, because unlimited liability for data breaches is a mandatory term in contracts for sensitive data","Accept the terms, because under shared responsibility the provider is responsible for securing the customer's data","Rely on the liability clause as the main way of transferring the financial risk of a breach to the cloud provider"],"a":[0],"e":"Liability caps mean contractual transfer is limited. The organization keeps most of the impact, so it needs strong technical controls plus insurance. Providers rarely accept unlimited liability, and under shared responsibility the customer still secures its data and configuration."},
{"id":"g41","d":1,"q":"Which activity is the PRIMARY purpose of a security architecture review board?","o":["Govern conformance to security principles","Perform penetration tests of new systems before they go live and report the findings to the project teams","Write all of the organization's security policies and standards and keep them aligned with regulations","Run the security operations center and coordinate the response to security incidents across the business"],"a":[0],"e":"An architecture review board provides governance over design: conformance with principles, standards, and approved patterns, with a path for deviations. Testing, policy writing, and SOC operations are other functions."},
{"id":"g42","d":1,"q":"A company processes biometric data (fingerprint templates) for employee time tracking in a jurisdiction with a biometric privacy law requiring written notice, consent, and a published retention schedule. What should the architect ensure FIRST?","o":["That it can't go live until notice, consent, and the retention and destruction schedule are built in","That the biometric templates are stored on an encrypted file share accessible only to the HR team","That the time-tracking vendor contractually commits to a 99.9% availability service level agreement","That the templates are hashed with SHA-256 before storage so that the raw fingerprints can't be recovered"],"a":[0],"e":"Laws like Illinois BIPA impose consent and retention duties with statutory damages per violation. These legal requirements must be built in before collection starts. Encryption is good but doesn't satisfy consent. Note that you can't simply hash biometric templates, since matching needs fuzzy comparison."},
{"id":"g43","d":1,"q":"An organization's privacy office requires a Data Protection Impact Assessment (DPIA) for new projects. Which project MOST clearly requires one under GDPR?","o":["Systematic monitoring of employees' behavior and productivity using AI-driven analytics across the workforce","Upgrading the operating system on the internal web servers that host the corporate intranet","Migrating the internal wiki to a newer version of the same product with the same content","Replacing printers in the corporate office with new multifunction devices that scan to email"],"a":[0],"e":"GDPR Article 35 requires a DPIA for processing likely to result in high risk, including systematic monitoring and new technologies that evaluate people. The other changes don't involve high-risk processing of personal data."},
{"id":"g44","d":1,"q":"A security architect is designing a records management solution. Several jurisdictions require tax records to be kept for different periods: 5, 7, and 10 years. The company wants a single global retention rule. What is the BEST approach?","o":["Apply the retention period required by each jurisdiction to its records, and avoid keeping them longer unless a legal hold applies","Keep every record for ten years, since this satisfies the longest requirement and is the simplest rule to enforce","Keep every record for five years, since this minimizes storage costs and satisfies most of the jurisdictions","Keep every record indefinitely in low-cost archive storage, in case it is needed for future litigation or audit"],"a":[0],"e":"Retaining too short breaks the law; retaining too long increases exposure in breaches and discovery, and may break privacy laws. A single 10-year rule might be defensible for non-personal data but over-retains personal data elsewhere. Retention should follow the applicable requirement."},
{"id":"g45","d":1,"q":"After an audit, the organization receives a finding that its access reviews are performed but not documented. The application owner says the reviews do happen. What is the BEST response?","o":["Redesign the review process so that it produces evidence automatically, such as attestations in the IGA tool","Have the application owner sign a letter confirming the reviews were performed, and give it to the auditors","Formally dispute the finding, since the control did operate and the lack of documentation is only a formality","Suspend access reviews until an identity governance tool is purchased, then restart them with proper records"],"a":[0],"e":"Auditors rely on evidence; an undocumented control is, for assurance purposes, not operating. Build evidence into the process. An after-the-fact letter is weak evidence, and stopping the reviews makes the situation worse."},
{"id":"g46","d":1,"q":"An organization adopts a 'secure by design' principle in its enterprise architecture. Which practice MOST directly demonstrates this principle in governance?","o":["Security requirements are approved at concept stage, with architecture gates","A penetration test is performed by an independent firm before every production release of every application","A web application firewall is deployed in front of every new application before it is exposed to users","Annual secure coding training is mandatory for every developer before they can commit code to production"],"a":[0],"e":"Secure by design means security is built in from the start, which governance enforces through early requirements and design gates. Pen tests are late verification, firewalls are a control, and training is an enabler."},
{"id":"g47","d":1,"q":"A security architect is asked to evaluate the residual risk of a new design. Which formula BEST represents the concept?","o":["Residual risk equals inherent risk minus the risk reduction achieved by the controls that are in place","Residual risk = threat x vulnerability x asset value, calculated before controls are applied","Residual risk = total risk + the control gap, where the gap is the cost of the countermeasures","Residual risk = annualized loss expectancy x annualized rate of occurrence for each threat"],"a":[0],"e":"Residual risk is what remains after controls. Threat x vulnerability x value is a common expression of inherent risk, 'total risk + control gap' is a CISSP-style shorthand where residual = total risk minus countermeasures plus the gap, and ALE already includes ARO."},
{"id":"g48","d":1,"q":"Which BEST describes the difference between a regulation and a framework from a security architect's perspective?","o":["Regulations bind by law; frameworks don't","A regulation applies only to government agencies, while a framework applies only to private companies","A framework becomes legally binding as soon as it is adopted, while a regulation is advisory guidance","There is no practical difference, because both are optional unless a customer contract requires them"],"a":[0],"e":"Regulations carry legal force. Frameworks (NIST CSF, ISO 27001, COBIT) are adopted voluntarily, although contracts or regulators sometimes require them, which can make them binding in that context."},
{"id":"g49","d":1,"q":"A company is preparing to adopt generative AI tools for employees. Legal is concerned about confidential data being sent to third-party AI services. Which governance control should the architect recommend FIRST?","o":["An AI acceptable use policy with data rules for each service, backed by approved, contractually vetted tools","Block all generative AI websites at the web proxy so that employees can't send company data to any AI service","Require every employee to sign a new non-disclosure agreement that specifically covers the use of AI tools","Encrypt all files on employee laptops so that confidential documents can't be uploaded to third-party services"],"a":[0],"e":"Governance starts with clear rules and sanctioned options. Blanket blocking drives shadow use, NDAs don't address third-party processing, and laptop encryption doesn't stop users pasting data into a website."},
{"id":"g50","d":1,"q":"During a risk assessment, the architect finds that a single administrator manages all network devices and holds the only copy of the configuration passwords. Which risk does this MOST directly represent?","o":["Key person dependency combined with a lack of separation of duties and dual control","Excessive network segmentation between the device management networks","Insufficient encryption of configuration files on the network devices","Weak physical security controls in the network equipment rooms"],"a":[0],"e":"One person with sole control creates availability risk (they leave or are unavailable) and integrity risk (no oversight). Remedies include a password vault, shared break-glass procedures, and dual control."},
{"id":"g51","d":1,"q":"An organization is assessing a cloud provider. The provider will not allow customer-performed audits but offers ISO 27001, SOC 2 Type II, and CSA STAR Level 2 attestations. What is the BEST approach?","o":["Rely on the attestations after checking their scope and exceptions, and close any gaps by other means","Reject the provider, since a contractual right to audit the provider directly is mandatory for any critical service","Accept the provider without further review, since three independent certifications demonstrate adequate security","Perform an unannounced audit of the provider's facilities anyway, to verify the claims made in the attestations"],"a":[0],"e":"Large providers rarely allow individual audits and offer third-party attestations instead. The architect must check scope (are the services you use covered?), exceptions, and the carve-outs for subservice organizations. Accepting certifications without review ignores those gaps."},
{"id":"g52","d":1,"q":"Which of the following is the BEST indicator that security governance is effective in an organization?","o":["Risk is part of business decisions","The security team receives a large and growing budget that is approved by the board every year","No security incidents were reported to management during the past twelve months of operations","Every employee completed the mandatory security awareness training course within the past year"],"a":[0],"e":"Good governance is visible in how decisions are made. Budget is an input, zero reported incidents may mean poor detection, and training completion is a compliance metric."},
{"id":"g53","d":1,"q":"A security architect must recommend a risk treatment for a highly unlikely but catastrophic event (for example, the loss of a primary data center from a regional disaster). Controls to fully mitigate it are very expensive. Which approach is MOST appropriate?","o":["Combine partial mitigation, such as a warm site, with insurance, aligned to the BIA's recovery objectives","Accept the risk, because the probability of losing the entire data center to a regional disaster is very low","Avoid the risk by shutting down the primary data center and moving all workloads to a single cloud region","Fully mitigate the risk with active-active data centers in separate regions, regardless of the cost involved"],"a":[0],"e":"Low-probability, high-impact risks often call for a mix: enough mitigation to meet business recovery needs plus insurance for the financial impact. Simple acceptance ignores catastrophic impact, and full mitigation may cost more than it saves."},
{"id":"g54","d":1,"q":"A company's development team wants to use production customer data in the test environment to improve realism. The test environment has weaker controls. What should the architect recommend?","o":["Use masked, synthetic, or tokenized data in test, and if real data is truly unavoidable, apply production-level controls with approval","Allow production data in the test environment as long as every developer with access signs a non-disclosure agreement","Copy production data into the test environment but delete it automatically every 30 days to limit exposure","Encrypt the test database using the same key as production, so the data is protected the same way in both"],"a":[0],"e":"Production data in weakly controlled environments is a common breach source. Masked or synthetic data removes the risk. If real data is truly needed, the environment must be protected to production standard. NDAs and periodic deletion don't fix exposure during use."},
{"id":"g55","d":1,"q":"An organization is subject to a breach notification law requiring notification unless the compromised data was encrypted and the key was not compromised. Which architecture decision MOST directly reduces notification obligations?","o":["Encrypt sensitive data with strong algorithms and keep the keys separate, under strict and independent access control","Add more firewall rules at the perimeter to reduce the likelihood that attackers reach the database","Shorten log retention so that less information is available to attackers if the systems are compromised","Require longer, more complex passwords on the database servers and the database administrator accounts"],"a":[0],"e":"Many breach laws include an encryption safe harbor if keys aren't compromised. Separating keys from data (HSM or KMS with separate access) is what makes the safe harbor apply. Firewalls and passwords reduce the likelihood of a breach but don't change notification obligations once it happens."},
{"id":"g56","d":1,"q":"A security architect is working with a startup that has no formal security program. The CEO wants to 'get compliant' to win enterprise customers who are asking for evidence of security. Budget is limited. Which approach should the architect recommend?","o":["Identify which assurance customers actually require (such as SOC 2 Type II or ISO 27001), perform a gap assessment against it, and prioritize by risk","Buy a comprehensive security platform covering every control domain, then pursue every available certification at once so the sales team has the broadest possible coverage","Write a full set of security policies copied from a template library and send them to customers as evidence of a mature security program","Wait until the company has more revenue, since security programs are only cost-effective at larger scale and customers will usually accept a promise to address it later"],"a":[0],"e":"Start from the business driver: what assurance do customers need? A focused gap assessment and risk-based roadmap uses limited budget well. Copied policies without implementation are a liability, and buying tools before defining requirements wastes money."},
{"id":"g57","d":1,"q":"Two business units disagree about who owns a shared customer database. Marketing says it owns the data because it runs campaigns; Sales says it owns it because it enters most records. No one has approved access requests for months. What should the architect recommend?","o":["Have senior management formally assign a single data owner accountable for classification and access, with data stewards in each unit","Assign ownership to IT since IT runs the database servers, backs up the data, and is in the best position to understand the technical controls that protect it","Let both business units share ownership equally, with each one approving the access requests that relate to its own staff and use cases","Freeze all access to the database until the business units reach agreement among themselves through their normal planning cycle"],"a":[0],"e":"Accountability needs a single owner, assigned by management. IT is the custodian, not the owner. Joint ownership produces exactly the gap described, and freezing access disrupts business without solving the governance issue."},
{"id":"g58","d":1,"q":"Which statement BEST describes the relationship between a security architecture and the enterprise architecture?","o":["Security architecture is an integral view of the enterprise architecture, aligned with business strategy and addressing security across business, data, application, and technology layers","Security architecture is a separate document maintained by the security team that the enterprise architects consult only when a project has a regulatory component","Security architecture replaces the technology layer of the enterprise architecture with a set of security products and their configuration standards","Security architecture is limited to the network perimeter and the controls that filter traffic between the internal network and the internet"],"a":[0],"e":"Frameworks such as SABSA and TOGAF treat security as an integrated concern across all layers, driven by business requirements. Treating it as a bolt-on or as network-only leaves gaps."},
{"id":"g59","d":1,"q":"A company's internal audit function reports to the CIO. The audit committee is concerned about the independence of IT audit findings. What is the BEST recommendation?","o":["Have internal audit report functionally to the audit committee of the board, with administrative reporting to a senior executive outside IT","Have internal audit report to the CISO, since security is the most frequent subject of IT audit findings and the CISO understands them best","Outsource all IT audits to the external financial auditor and disband the internal audit team to remove the conflict entirely","Keep the current structure but require the CIO to sign every audit report before release so that findings are formally acknowledged"],"a":[0],"e":"Audit independence comes from reporting to the audit committee. Reporting to the CIO or CISO means auditing your own boss. Having the auditee sign off before release worsens the problem."},
{"id":"g60","d":1,"q":"A security architect is asked to establish metrics for the vulnerability management program. Which metric BEST reflects how effectively the organization is reducing exposure?","o":["Mean time to remediate critical vulnerabilities on internet-facing assets, compared against the defined SLA","Total number of vulnerability scans performed per quarter across all network segments and asset classes","Number of vulnerabilities discovered in total, including informational findings and low-severity configuration notes","Number of security patches released by vendors in the last month that apply to products in the environment"],"a":[0],"e":"Remediation time for high-risk exposures against an SLA measures outcomes. Scan counts and raw vulnerability totals measure activity and can go up when the program improves. Vendor patch counts are outside your control."},
{"id":"g61","d":1,"q":"A regulator requires that a bank's critical systems be tested with 'threat-led penetration testing' every three years. The bank's architect must plan for this. Which approach BEST aligns with the requirement?","o":["Engage testers who use current intelligence on actors targeting the sector to emulate realistic attacks against live production systems","Run an automated vulnerability scanner against all external IP addresses and submit the resulting report to the regulator","Ask the internal network team to review firewall rules against best practice and document the results for the regulator","Run a tabletop exercise with the executive team based on a fictional ransomware scenario and record the lessons learned"],"a":[0],"e":"Threat-led testing (TIBER-EU, DORA TLPT, CBEST) uses intelligence about real adversaries to drive red-team testing on production. Scans and rule reviews are not threat-led, and tabletops don't test technical defenses."},
{"id":"g62","d":1,"q":"A security architect discovers that the organization's risk register lists 400 risks, most rated 'medium,' with no clear owners. Executives say the register is 'not useful.' What is the BEST way to improve it?","o":["Consolidate into business-level risks with named owners and clear rating criteria, linked to treatment plans and to the risk appetite","Add more technical detail to each of the 400 risks so executives can understand exactly how each one could be exploited","Re-rate every risk as high so that executives pay attention and approve funding for the full security roadmap","Replace the register with the findings from the latest vulnerability scan, which are objective and easier to maintain"],"a":[0],"e":"Registers fail when they're long, unowned, and lack decision linkage. Aggregating to business-level risks with owners and appetite makes it actionable. More technical detail, inflated ratings, or a vulnerability list don't help governance."},
{"id":"g63","d":1,"q":"A company is moving to a cloud-first strategy. The board wants assurance that cloud risk is managed consistently across three providers. Which governance mechanism is MOST effective?","o":["A cloud security policy and standards with technical guardrails (policy-as-code) applied consistently across providers, plus continuous posture monitoring","Allow each cloud team to define its own controls since they understand their provider best, with a yearly summary report to the board","Require each provider to sign the organization's information security policy and treat that as sufficient assurance","Limit the organization to one provider so that only one set of controls is needed, regardless of the business need for multiple providers"],"a":[0],"e":"Consistent policy enforced through guardrails and monitored continuously gives the board real assurance. Team-by-team controls diverge, signatures don't enforce configuration, and dropping providers ignores business requirements."},
{"id":"g64","d":1,"q":"An architect must justify the cost of a data loss prevention program to the CFO. Which input is MOST relevant to calculating the expected benefit?","o":["The estimated reduction in annualized loss from exfiltration and accidental disclosure, based on incident data","The number of sensitive data patterns supported out of the box by the DLP product's default policy library","The list price of the DLP product compared against the average list price of the three nearest competitors","The number of endpoints, mail gateways, and web proxies that will have the DLP inspection agent installed"],"a":[0],"e":"Benefit is risk reduction in monetary terms. Feature counts, price comparisons, and deployment counts describe cost or scope, not benefit."},
{"id":"g65","d":1,"q":"A company in a heavily regulated sector wants to use a new SaaS analytics tool. The SaaS vendor uses several subprocessors, including one in a country with weak privacy protections. Which action is MOST important?","o":["Review the subprocessor list and data flows, assess the risk of each relevant subprocessor, and require contractual flow-down of obligations plus notice of changes","Accept the vendor because subprocessors are the vendor's responsibility under the contract and the organization's obligations end at the direct relationship","Require the vendor to remove all subprocessors before signing, regardless of their role in delivering the service","Ask the vendor to confirm in writing that it is compliant with all applicable regulations"],"a":[0],"e":"Fourth-party risk matters: data flowing to subprocessors carries your obligations. Flow-down terms and change notification are standard. Your accountability doesn't end at the direct vendor, removing all subprocessors is unrealistic, and a general compliance letter has little value."},
{"id":"g66","d":1,"q":"An organization adopts the 'three lines' model for risk governance. Where does the security architecture function typically sit?","o":["In the first or second line, depending on whether it builds controls or sets standards, but never the third","Always in the third line, because architects review designs independently of the teams that build them","Outside the model entirely, because architecture is a technical function rather than a risk function","In the third line when it reviews designs, and in the first line when it writes security policies"],"a":[0],"e":"First line owns and manages risk (operations, delivery). Second line sets frameworks and oversees (risk, compliance, often security governance). Third line is independent assurance (internal audit). Architecture designs controls or sets standards, so it can't be the independent third line."},
{"id":"g67","d":1,"q":"A health insurer is implementing a new claims system. The privacy officer insists on 'privacy by design.' Which design decision MOST directly demonstrates it?","o":["Collect only the data fields needed for claims adjudication, with default settings that restrict access and sharing, and build in retention and deletion","Encrypt the database with AES-256 and enable transparent data encryption on all tables, so that data is protected wherever it is stored","Add a privacy notice to the claims portal login page that explains how data will be used","Perform a penetration test of the claims system before go-live to confirm that privacy controls work"],"a":[0],"e":"Privacy by design is about minimization and privacy as the default, built into the system. Encryption protects confidentiality but doesn't limit collection or use. Notices and pen tests are useful but aren't design."},
{"id":"g68","d":1,"q":"An organization's crown-jewel intellectual property is stored in a document management system. A risk assessment finds that 3,000 employees have read access, although only 200 need it. What is the BEST risk treatment from an architecture perspective?","o":["Restrict access to the 200 who need it, monitor access, and have owners review it","Accept the risk, because every employee signed a confidentiality agreement as part of their employment contract","Move the IP to an encrypted file share protected by a single strong password shared with the 200 users who need it","Purchase an insurance policy that covers theft of intellectual property by employees and contractors"],"a":[0],"e":"Least privilege plus monitoring plus recurring review directly reduces exposure. NDAs don't prevent exfiltration, a shared password removes accountability, and insurance rarely covers the real value of lost IP."},
{"id":"g69","d":1,"q":"The architect is preparing a security strategy roadmap for the next three years. Which input should drive the prioritization MOST?","o":["The organization's business strategy and risk profile, including planned initiatives and the risks above appetite","The latest security products recommended by analysts and the features released by current vendors","The security team's preferences and the technologies the team is most familiar with","The list of controls from the most recent compliance framework adopted by a competitor"],"a":[0],"e":"Security strategy follows business strategy and risk. Products, team preferences, and competitor frameworks are secondary inputs."},
{"id":"g70","d":1,"q":"A security architect is designing an approach for managing the security of acquired companies. Each acquisition has different maturity levels and technologies. Which is the BEST governance mechanism?","o":["An integration playbook with minimum baselines, risk-based timelines, and checkpoints before deeper integration","Require every acquired company to replace all of its technology with the parent company's standard stack before day one","Let acquired companies operate independently with no new requirements, since they were successful before being acquired","Require every acquired company to obtain the same security certifications as the parent company within 30 days"],"a":[0],"e":"A repeatable playbook with baselines and gated integration manages varying maturity. Full replacement before day one is impractical, full independence ignores inherited risk, and 30-day certification is unrealistic."},
{"id":"g71","d":1,"q":"Which document BEST communicates the specific security requirements that an outsourced application development vendor must follow?","o":["A contractually referenced schedule of secure development requirements, testing duties, acceptance criteria, and fix timelines","The organization's high-level information security policy, attached to the statement of work as a reference document","The vendor's own secure coding guidelines, which were reviewed and found acceptable during the sales process","A verbal agreement made during the project kickoff meeting, recorded in the minutes and sent to the vendor"],"a":[0],"e":"Specific, enforceable requirements belong in the contract with acceptance criteria. High-level policy is too vague, the vendor's own guidelines may not meet your needs, and meeting minutes aren't enforceable terms."},
{"id":"g72","d":1,"q":"A multinational company must design an approach for responding to lawful access requests from governments for customer data. Which design element is MOST important?","o":["A documented process with legal review, minimal disclosure, and transparency","A policy of complying immediately with every government request, to avoid any legal penalties in any jurisdiction","A policy of rejecting every government request for customer data, to protect customer privacy in all cases","Storing all customer data in a single country, so that only one government's legal process ever applies"],"a":[0],"e":"Lawful access requests involve conflicting laws. A governed process with legal review and minimization protects both customers and the company. Blanket compliance or refusal are both risky, and single-country storage may break residency laws elsewhere."},
{"id":"g73","d":1,"q":"A company has completed a risk assessment and decided to accept a risk. Which statement about risk acceptance is MOST accurate?","o":["Document it with the rationale, have an authorized owner approve it, then monitor it and re-evaluate it when conditions change","Once a risk is accepted it can be closed and removed from the register, since no further action is planned","Any manager may accept any risk on behalf of the organization, as long as the decision is documented","Accepting a risk transfers it to the business owner, who then becomes financially liable for any loss"],"a":[0],"e":"Acceptance is a conscious, documented decision by someone with authority appropriate to the risk level, revisited over time. It isn't transfer, and accepted risks stay on the register."},
{"id":"g74","d":1,"q":"A payment provider must meet PCI DSS v4.0. The assessor allows a 'customized approach' for a particular requirement. What does this mean for the architect?","o":["Meet the requirement's objective with its own control design, documented with a targeted risk analysis and proven effective","Skip the requirement entirely, provided that a business reason for not meeting it is documented and approved","Use only vendor products that are certified by the PCI Security Standards Council to meet the requirement","Mark the requirement as not applicable on the report on compliance, with the assessor's agreement"],"a":[0],"e":"PCI DSS v4.0's customized approach lets an entity meet the objective in a different way, with a controls matrix, targeted risk analysis, and evidence of effectiveness. It isn't an exemption."},
{"id":"g75","d":1,"q":"An architect is asked to assess whether a cloud-based HR system meets the requirement that 'personnel data must not be accessible to the provider's support staff without customer approval.' Which provider capability BEST addresses this?","o":["Customer lockbox approval, customer-managed keys, and logging of provider access","A SOC 2 report stating that all of the provider's support staff pass criminal background checks before hiring","TLS 1.3 encryption on every connection between users' browsers and the provider's HR application","Phishing-resistant multi-factor authentication for all of the organization's HR system administrators"],"a":[0],"e":"Lockbox-type features require customer approval before provider engineers access data, and customer keys plus access logs give enforcement and visibility. Background checks, TLS, and customer-side MFA don't control provider access."},
{"id":"g76","d":1,"q":"A security architect needs to evaluate the maturity of the organization's security processes to set a realistic improvement target. Which model is MOST appropriate?","o":["A capability maturity model, such as CMMI or the NIST CSF implementation tiers","A threat model, such as STRIDE, applied to each business process in turn","An authenticated vulnerability scan of every network asset in the environment","A quantitative risk model, such as FAIR, applied to the top ten business risks"],"a":[0],"e":"Maturity models measure process capability and give a path for improvement. STRIDE identifies threats, scanning finds technical flaws, and FAIR quantifies loss; none of them measure process maturity."},
{"id":"g77","d":1,"q":"A business owner wants to deploy a system that the security team has assessed as exceeding risk appetite. The business owner argues the revenue opportunity justifies it. Who should make the final decision?","o":["An executive or committee with authority at that risk level","The security architect, because the architect understands the technical risk and its consequences better than anyone else","The business owner alone, because it is the owner's system and the owner will benefit from the revenue it produces","The IT operations manager, because the operations team will have to run and support the system once it is deployed"],"a":[0],"e":"Risks exceeding appetite need escalation to whoever has authority to accept them, often the executive committee or board risk committee. The architect advises, and the business owner may not have authority to exceed appetite on their own."},
{"id":"g78","d":1,"q":"An organization must comply with a cybersecurity regulation that requires an annual attestation by the CEO that the cybersecurity program is effective. Which architecture capability BEST supports that attestation?","o":["A control assurance program that continuously collects evidence of control operation and reports exceptions to executives","A detailed network diagram signed off by the network team, showing all security controls at the perimeter","An annual external vulnerability scan report showing no critical findings on internet-facing systems","A complete library of security policies, reviewed and formally approved by the board of directors"],"a":[0],"e":"Executive attestations (for example, NYDFS Part 500) need evidence that controls operate. Continuous control monitoring with exception reporting gives the CEO a defensible basis. Diagrams, single scans, and policies alone don't show effectiveness."},
{"id":"g79","d":1,"q":"A security architect is planning to use the SABSA framework. Which layer would MOST directly capture the business drivers and risks that justify the architecture?","o":["The contextual layer, which represents the business view and asks 'what' the business needs","The physical layer, which represents the builder's view of mechanisms and technologies","The component layer, which represents the tradesman's view of specific products and tools","The operational layer, which represents service management and how the system runs day to day"],"a":[0],"e":"SABSA's contextual layer captures business context, drivers, and assets. The conceptual layer then defines attributes (the 'business attributes profile'), with logical, physical, component, and operational layers following."},
{"id":"g80","d":1,"q":"Which scenario BEST illustrates risk transference?","o":["Contracting with a provider that indemnifies breaches it causes through negligence, plus buying cyber insurance","Installing a web application firewall in front of a vulnerable application that can't be patched yet","Decommissioning an unused legacy server rather than spending effort to patch and maintain it","Documenting a decision to proceed with a project without any additional controls being added"],"a":[0],"e":"Transference shifts the financial impact to another party through contracts or insurance. WAF is mitigation, decommissioning is avoidance, and proceeding without controls is acceptance."},
{"id":"g81","d":1,"q":"An organization needs to demonstrate to a regulator that its security controls are effective, not just present. Which type of evidence is STRONGEST?","o":["Independent testing showing the control operating as intended over a period, such as sampled audit tests","A policy requiring the control, approved by the executive team and communicated to all staff","A screenshot of the control's configuration, taken by the auditor during the on-site visit","A written statement from the system administrator confirming that the control is enabled"],"a":[0],"e":"Independent testing over time shows operating effectiveness. Policies show intent, a single screenshot shows design at a moment, and self-attestation is the weakest evidence."},
{"id":"g82","d":1,"q":"A security architect is designing a governance process for cloud service adoption. Teams complain that the current security review takes eight weeks and blocks innovation. Which change BEST balances speed and risk?","o":["Pre-approved patterns with guardrails for a fast track","Remove the review process entirely, and rely on each team to self-assess the security of its own cloud designs","Add more reviewers to the existing process so that every design still receives a full review, only faster","Require every team to use a single approved cloud service, so that design reviews are no longer necessary"],"a":[0],"e":"Approved patterns and guardrails shift security left and allow fast, safe adoption. Removing review loses oversight, more reviewers doesn't scale, and limiting services ignores business needs."},
{"id":"g83","d":1,"q":"An organization stores personal data of minors through an educational app. Several jurisdictions have rules specific to children's data. Which requirement is MOST likely to drive architectural change?","o":["Verifiable parental consent before collection, with age-appropriate defaults and strict limits on profiling","Encryption of all children's data at rest using a FIPS 140-3 validated cryptographic module","Multi-factor authentication for every child user, enforced at each login to the educational app","Storage of all children's data in a single regional data center in the company's home country"],"a":[0],"e":"Laws like COPPA and the UK Age Appropriate Design Code require parental consent and privacy-protective defaults. These change data flows and features. MFA for children is impractical and not required."},
{"id":"g84","d":1,"q":"A security architect wants to make sure that architecture decisions and their risk rationale are preserved for future teams and auditors. Which practice is MOST effective?","o":["Architecture decision records capturing context, options, decision, consequences, and the risk accepted","Recordings of all architecture review meetings, stored on a shared drive for future reference","The experience of senior architects, who can be consulted to recall past decisions when needed","Final design diagrams only, since they show the outcome of each decision in a clear visual form"],"a":[0],"e":"Architecture decision records preserve why decisions were made, not just what. Recordings are hard to search, memory leaves with people, and diagrams show outcomes, not rationale."},
{"id":"g85","d":1,"q":"A regulator finds that a company did not adequately assess the security of a critical supplier that later suffered a breach exposing the company's customer data. Which principle has the company MOST likely violated?","o":["Due diligence","Separation of duties, by allowing the supplier to process the data","Least privilege, by granting the supplier access to customer data","Defense in depth, by relying on a single supplier for a critical service"],"a":[0],"e":"Due diligence is the investigation and verification that inform a decision and the ongoing oversight of it. Failing to assess the supplier is a due diligence failure. The other principles are technical controls, not governance obligations."}
);
